          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is anemia, and does it always mean low iron?",
          "answer": "**What anemia is:** Anemia means a lower-than-normal amount of hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. (*Source: Iron-deficiency anemia, general clinical reference.*)\n\n**Does it always mean low iron?** No. The sources say iron deficiency is the most common cause of anemia worldwide, but not the only one. The passages I found did not describe the other causes, so I can't tell you what they are from these sources.\n\n**Why low iron still matters:** The same source says that in an adult who is not having heavy periods or pregnant, low iron is a sign to look for where the iron has gone, because the body doesn't lose much iron except through bleeding. A gastrointestinal bleeding guidance note adds that iron-deficiency anemia can be a reason to check for bleeding in the digestive tract. It also notes that black, tarry stool can be a sign of bleeding higher up, and that iron supplements can darken stool too. (*Source: Gastrointestinal bleeding, society guidance.*)\n\nIf you have been told you have anemia, or you have symptoms such as tiredness, pale skin, or blood or black stool, bring these questions to a clinician. They can work out the cause from blood tests and your history. This is general information, not a diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
